<div class="section-eyebrow">The voyage</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">Take one hypothetical Suezmax and follow it from the load port to the discharge port.</h2>

<p class="prose">The Hormuz bypass is usually described as a pipeline and a port. One cargo shows that it is a chain. Take a hypothetical Suezmax loading about one million barrels of Saudi crude at Yanbu, on the Red Sea, for Ulsan in South Korea. Bab el-Mandeb has been under a Houthi blockade since 20 July 2026, so the vessel cannot go south. It goes north through the Suez Canal, west through the Mediterranean, out past Gibraltar, down the Atlantic coast of Africa, around the Cape of Good Hope, and east across the Indian Ocean to the Strait of Malacca and on to Korea.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-label">What this analysis does and does not claim</div>
  <div class="callout-body">The vessel is hypothetical. This page does not track any ship, and it does not forecast whether any chokepoint will close or reopen. The distances come from the Narrows routing graph, the same network used by our Map Printer tool, and the loss figures come from CAPE's default assumptions. Where a market figure comes from a press report, it is attributed, and where reports disagree, the page says so.</div>
</div>

